  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front drops southward this morning with \nincreasing clouds and much cooler temperatures. Some light rain is \nforecast across the northern half of the area.\n\nSurface observations show a cold front translating southward across \nthe area as high pressure builds in from the NW. Satellite shows \nquite a bit of mid level cloud cover moving into the region. Radar \nshows some very light returns in the Piedmont but little if any \nprecip is making it to the ground. Expect the front to continue \nsouthward through the morning with winds becoming NE as high \npressure to the north allows for CAD to develop across the region. \nBecoming a bit breezy near the coast today. 00z guidance has \ncontinued the trend of shaving a degree or two off of previously \nforecast temperatures behind this backdoor front. Highs range from \nthe mid 60s N, upper 60s to low 70s along S of I-64, and upper 70s \nto low 80s near and S of the VA/NC border. High temps will occur in \nthe morning for the northern half of the area with temps holding \nsteady or slowly falling thereafter as CAD strengthens. It still \nappears that there will be an uptick in precip coverage across the \nNW and N this morning into the afternoon. QPF will be light with 0.1-\n0.15\" across the north, tapering to a few hundredths near Richmond \nand little to nothing south of US-460. Some light rain or drizzle \nmay linger into tonight, mainly for the Northern Neck and Eastern \nShore. Low temps range from the upper 50s NW to the mid/upper 60s \nSE. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and breezy Friday with gusty showers along the \nfront Friday night into Saturday. Much cooler temperatures arrive \nthis weekend.\n\nToday's front will lift northward as a warm front on Friday, \nscouring out the low level stable CAD airmass as an upper trough \ndigs into the Midwest and strengthens a surface low over the eastern \nGreat Lakes. High temps rebound into the mid and even upper 80s with \nbreezy SW winds. It now appears that the daylight hours of Friday \nwill be dry with the 00z models continuing to slow the progression \nof the strong front. PoPs increase after sunset, along and ahead of \nthe surging cold front. Hi-res guidance shows the potential for thin \nstrongly-forced line of convection along the front Friday evening. \nWith the somewhat slower frontal timing, even less instability is \navailable than it looked 24 hours ago. However, strong kinematics \naloft will likely be able to mix down to the surface with wind gusts \n30-40 mph possible as the front comes through. SPC has maintained a \nMarginal Risk (level 1/5) across the the N and NE portions of the \narea for strong to severe wind gusts. There will be little if any \nthunder with these showers despite the potential for strong wind \ngusts. \n\nModels have continued to trend toward a more amplified upper level \npattern across the SE CONUS this weekend. The NAM and ECMWF are the \nmost bullish, developing a closed upper low and coastal surface low \nwhile the GFS/Canadian models are much more progressive. This \nbimodal split in the guidance leads to significant uncertainty \nregarding the sensible weather this weekend and likely well into \nnext week. Regardless, it still appears that much colder air will \nspill into the area Saturday and Sunday with high temps in the mid \n50s NW to the low/mid 60s SE. Rainfall chances and placement are \nwhere the highest uncertainty lies. The amplified solutions would \nresult in rainfall continuing on Saturday and lingering potentially \ninto early next week as low pressure lingers offshore. Have trended \nPoPs and QPF toward the coastal low scenario but with very low \nconfidence at this time. Cold overnight lows Saturday night still \nappear on track but with more wind likely to diminish the potential \nfor frost, even in the typically colder Piedmont spots.",
